<Layout title="ETF Field Guide: Reading Costs Before Comparing Funds" description="Read the annual expense ratio together with trading costs and fund structure. Read the scope, assumptions and source boundaries behind ETF News Daily’s transpar"><article class="paper"><h1>Read the fine print.</h1><p class="lead">Read the annual expense ratio together with trading costs and fund structure.</p><p>Updated October 16, 2026</p><p>A 0.20% annual expense ratio is 20 basis points, not 20%.</p><p>In a simplified no-growth model, 10,000 held for one year at that fee ends at 9,980.</p><p>Real funds deduct expenses through assets; investors do not usually receive a separate annual invoice.</p><p>A zero commission trade can still cross a bid–ask spread.</p><p>A bid of 99.90 and ask of 100.10 around a midpoint of 100 imply a 0.20% full spread.</p><p>Buying and immediately selling at unchanged quotes loses 0.20 per share, before other costs.</p><p>Tracking difference measures realized return difference against a specified benchmark; tracking error describes variability of that difference.</p><p>Neither is interchangeable with the stated expense ratio.</p><p>The index may use different dividend or withholding conventions.</p><p>Compare like with like.</p><p>A registration amendment may update an existing fund rather than establish a new launch.</p><p>Read the cover, effective date, fee table, strategy, risks and share-class details before treating the filing as a product announcement.</p><p>Ten long-form sourced guides remain in editorial preparation and are not represented as published.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><p><a href="/tools/">Explore the cost models</a> · <a href="/filings/">Inspect SEC filings</a></p><p>Official reference: <a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/exchange-traded-funds-etfs">SEC Investor.gov: exchange-traded funds</a></p></article></Layout>